%%vskip 1.0cm
%%sep 0.2cm 0.2cm 7cm
%%vskip 0.8cm
%
W: 1. The trees all are bare, not one green leaf to be seen,
W:    And the meadows their beauty have lost.
W:    Now winter has come and 'tis cold for man and beast,
W:    And the streams they are frozen,
W:    The streams they are frozen fast by the frost.
W:
W: 2. 'Twas down in the farmyard where the oxen feed on straw,
W:    They send forth their breath like the steam.
W:    Sweet Betsy the milkmaid now quickly she must go,
W:    For the flakes of ice she finds,
W:    For the flakes of ice she finds a-floating on her cream.
W:
W: 3. 'Tis now all the small birds to the barn-door fly for food,
W:    And gently they rest on the spray.
W:    The innocent hares run from dens to search for food,
W:    Lest their footsteps should betray
W:    Lest their footsteps should their whereabouts betray
W:
W: 4. Now Christmas is come and our song is almost done,
W:    For soon shall come the turn of the year.
W:    So fill up your glasses and drink health all around,
W:    For we wish you all
W:    For we wish you all a bright and joyful New Year
%
%%vskip 1.0cm
%%sep 0.2cm 0.2cm 7cm
%%vskip 0.8cm
%
%%textfont Times-Italic 14
%%begintext fill
%%Unknown outside of Sussex until recent times, this song appears to derive from 'Winter', a poem written
%%by Thomas Brerewood of Horton, Cheshire (d. 1748). Known traditional singers include
%%the Downs shepherd, Michael Blann from Upper Beeding, whose MS songbook is in the
%%Sussex Library; George Townshend of Lewes, Sussex who was recorded in the 1960s; and
%%the Copper Family from Rottingdean, Sussex. Roud Index 1170.
%%endtext
%
%%vskip 0.3cm
%%textfont Times-Italic 14
%%begintext fill
%%Our lyrics are an amalgam of the words sung by Bob Copper on the Copper Family's
%%1971 recording, "A Song For Every Season" with a version recorded on Jackie Oates
%%& John Spiers 2024 recording, "A Midwinter's Night". The tune hews close to the 
%%Copper Family's version.
%%endtext
